<UserControl x:Class="Bws.Gui.FilterRow"
             xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
             xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
             Focusable="False"
             IsTabStop="False">

    <!--
        HOW THE WINDOW ASKS - the chips, their group labels and the button saying which columns the
        list shows. It stood in MainWindow.xaml until 2026-08-13, moved into the theme as a style
        carrying a template the same day (backlog 182), and became a UserControl the day after
        (backlog 185) once the details panel showed what the right shape was.

        THE SEAM IS A SUBJECT RATHER THAN A LINE COUNT: this row is how the window ASKS, and the
        rest of MainWindow.xaml is what the window IS.

        WHY IT IS A UserControl AND NOT A TEMPLATE, WHICH IS THE OWNER'S DECISION OF 2026-08-13
        AFTER THE PANEL MADE THE CHOICE VISIBLE. A theme file has no code-behind class, so a button
        living there had to be found by name at construction, refused with an exception when the
        part was missing, and have its click taken by the window - three mechanisms for one button.
        A UserControl has a class of its own, so the button is a field and the click is a handler,
        and the theme gets its hundred and fifty lines back. One mechanism for "a part of the window
        in its own file" rather than two that have to be told apart.

        Focusable and IsTabStop are off because a UserControl IS a ContentControl and a
        ContentControl is BOTH BY DEFAULT - measured, not assumed. Without them this row is a tab
        stop that does nothing, in front of the chips it holds. `docs/10` trap 8.

        THE CLICKABLE FILTERS, A5. Each one stands for one member of the query, and clicking it
        writes that member into the box above - which is the promise: the filter you clicked is a
        query you can read, edit and learn from. The tooltip shows the member before the click
        rather than after, so the box is not the only place it appears.

        AN ItemsControl RATHER THAN EIGHT CONTROLS WRITTEN OUT. The chips are data - which ones
        there are is decided in FilterChips.cs, where the reason for each is written beside it - and
        markup listing them again would be a second copy that drifts. It also means the row that
        account and signature chips will join is already built.

        WrapPanel, so a narrow window stacks them instead of clipping them. The count line beside
        the search box is the only thing in this window allowed to be cut, because it carries a
        message of the system's own length.

        THE TWO AutomationId VALUES BELOW ARE ON THE CONTROLS, NOT ON THE GRID, AND THAT IS A
        MEASURED CORRECTION. An id was put on the Grid first, and automation never saw it: WPF
        builds a peer for controls, not for layout panels, so the identifier sat on an element that
        is not in the tree at all. tools/window-journey needs to tell the labels of controls apart
        from the sentences the window says about the answer, and it does that by identity rather
        than by where things sit - the same lesson that file has now learnt three times.
    -->

    <Grid>
        <Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
            <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto" />
            <ColumnDefinition Width="*" />
            <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto" />
        </Grid.ColumnDefinitions>

        <ItemsControl Grid.Column="1"
                      AutomationProperties.AutomationId="filterChips"
                      ItemsSource="{Binding FilterGroups}"
                      ItemTemplate="{StaticResource FilterGroupTemplate}">
            <ItemsControl.ItemsPanel>
                <ItemsPanelTemplate>
                    <WrapPanel />
                </ItemsPanelTemplate>
            </ItemsControl.ItemsPanel>
        </ItemsControl>

        <!--
            WHICH COLUMNS THE LIST SHOWS, `A8`. A VISIBLE CONTROL RATHER THAN A MENU HIDDEN UNDER
            THE COLUMN HEADINGS, and that is `docs/11` rather than taste: section 6 grades
            "recognition rather than recall" as one of this window's two weakest heuristics, and a
            feature reachable only by right clicking something is a feature you have to already
            know about. The filters made the same choice one slice earlier - complaint 7 was closed
            with a visible row and a group label, not with a menu.

            The list ITSELF is a menu, which is the opposite decision and is about surface rather
            than discoverability: a panel of our own would need a fill, an edge and both of their
            contrast ratios argued, for a thing somebody opens a few times. The theme already
            dresses a menu and this window already uses one to copy a name.

            It sits at the end of the filters row rather than beside the search box, because what it
            changes is the list rather than the question - and the count line, which answers the
            question, already owns the right-hand end of the row above.

            Its own AutomationId, because tools/window-journey tells the labels of controls apart
            from the sentences the window says about the answer, and it does that by identity. This
            is the fourth time that lesson has been paid for in this row.
        -->
        <Button Grid.Column="2"
                x:Name="ColumnsButton"
                AutomationProperties.AutomationId="columnsButton"
                Content="{DynamicResource gui.columns.button}"
                ToolTip="{DynamicResource gui.columns.hint}"
                Click="ChooseColumns">
            <Button.ContextMenu>
                <!--
                    A CEILING AND A SCROLLBAR, because eighteen items is taller than a short screen
                    and a menu that runs off the bottom loses the last of them with nothing saying
                    so. The headings come from a flat list built in the window - see its
                    constructor.
                -->
                <ContextMenu MaxHeight="{StaticResource HeightMenu}"
                             ScrollViewer.VerticalScrollBarVisibility="Auto" />
            </Button.ContextMenu>
        </Button>
    </Grid>
</UserControl>
